egin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 3 e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}+\\left(- 12 x e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 x \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =36 x^{2} e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 12 x e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 3 e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\\\  & =3 \\left(12 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{4 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186526457 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526448 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526448 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=- x^{2} - 2 x y$ and $y=- x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8295975-e0c2-5952-8fb4-2cd0c1975736" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526448 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x - 2 y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1x) & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}) & =2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-1\\cdot \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)+\\left(-1x\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =4 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 4 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x \\\\  & =2 x \\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186526432 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526455 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526455 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=- 3 x e^{4 y}$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dab73ae4-4bf3-5f01-8c7a-eff6d6a066f0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526455 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 3 e^{4 y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 12 x e^{4 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 3 e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 12 x e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 3 e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}+\\left(- 12 x e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 36 x e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 3 e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}} \\\\  & =- 3 \\left(12 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} + 1\\right) e^{4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526461 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186166748.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526461 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "786f911d-355f-5878-9cda-bf6174bb6545" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529474 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=- 3 x - 3 y,$ where $x=- s - 2 t$ and $y=3 s + 3 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c54d338-de07-59a2-8a30-feaf06d318af" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529474 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=-3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-3, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-2, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot (3) \\\\  & =(6)+(-9) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529472 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529477 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=3 x + y,$ where $x=- 2 s - 3 t$ and $y=s - 3 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a433498-d0db-5e2d-80cc-422f4772f109" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529477 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(3)\\cdot (-3)+(1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =(-9)+(-3) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529480 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529470 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529480 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529480 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=2 x - 3 y,$ where $x=s - t$ and $y=2 s - 2 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "592d650a-7aa0-5957-9d77-7248908a6bf8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529480 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =(2)+(-6) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529493 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529488 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=- 2 x - 3 y,$ where $x=3 s - t$ and $y=- s - 2 t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d541dba-0895-564d-b1f6-b95dbb99a330" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529488 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (3)+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =(-6)+(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529501 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529494 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529494 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=x - 3 y,$ where $x=- s + 2 t$ and $y=s + t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc7d76dd-c18f-5702-856b-f5311bdfc8e8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529494 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot (2)+(-3)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(2)+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529504 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186166749.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186529504 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "4903629d-ed9b-55f5-87b9-195d27eeb2e1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186532756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531934 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "822bcaa0-c52b-52f8-b781-cc9986a54dc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532063 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532063 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532063 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3de248ef-eae0-53e3-95b5-fbeb5926dd60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532151 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532151 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532151 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61bbc39f-0989-5704