$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\&=\\left|\\left[\\begin{matrix}1 & 0\\\\2 x & 3\\end{matrix}\\right]\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u^{2}}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{x^{2} + x + 3 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_D e^{x+(x^{2} + 3 y)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{u + v}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{u + v}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{1}^{3} e^{u} e^{v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(e^{u}\\right)[\\in